      "keyboard": "Enter",
      "width": 320,
      "heading": "03 · Watch save and restore",
      "destinationText": "0303 · Watch save and restoreQuestion: what changes after consuming one number or symbol? Choosing a preset restarts this experiment. Keep the expression fixed, advance one item, and compare the marked characters, before/after totals and saved pairs. Rewind to check restoration.Invariant (a fact preserved at each step): total equals the sum of consumed terms at the current parenthesis level. Each saved (outer total, outer sign) pair records a group still awaiting its merge. When a number or group begins, sign is its multiplier, not the sign of the final answer. After a closing parenthesis it is temporarily unused until another operator resets it.This is a simulation of algorithm states, not execution of the Python below. Frames follow complete operations: digit runs are read together, spaces are skipped, and no other operations are omitted. Teaching snapshots are separate from submission memory.Expression preset (change restarts)8-(3-1)-(2+3)12 - (4 + 5)(1+(4+5+2)-3)+(6+8)1-(-2)0 2-1 + 2 1 + 1← Undo last itemConsume next item →Reset to startCompleted operations (scrub to revisit)Operation 0/7; character offsets start at 0Input characters: ␠ denotes a space; a thick solid border marks the item just consumed; dotted borders mark pending characters.80-1(233-415)6Current-level total = 0; stored sign = 1; saved levels = 0Start: total=0, sign=+1, stack empty.Saved contexts (oldest above, stack top below)No saved contextsCumulative operation ledgerEach row records a completed operation. Before and after states surround that row’s operation; future results are hidden.Offset / itemBefore total / signAfter total / signSaved levels",
